title: "Keep two analyses apart in named R scopes"
output: rmarkdown::html_vignette
vignette: >
  %\VignetteIndexEntry{Keep two analyses apart in named R scopes}
  %\VignetteEngine{knitr::rmarkdown}
  %\VignetteEncoding{UTF-8}
---

```{r setup, include = FALSE}
source("tools/guides/setup.R")
```

You want an agent to work on two unrelated analyses in one R session without
their objects colliding. The R endpoint keeps a separate persistent
environment for each named scope. `scopes` lists what each holds, reporting an
active binding without running it, and `reset` discards one scope while the
others keep theirs.

## Two analyses side by side

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
agent_task("
  Keep two analyses apart in separate R scopes. In scope `cars`, fit
  `mpg ~ wt` on `datasets::mtcars` and add a read-only active binding `slope`
  that returns the fitted slope. In scope `flowers`, summarise mean
  `Petal.Length` by `Species` in `datasets::iris`. Record what each scope
  holds, as the endpoint lists it, in the SQL workspace table `scope_contents`
  with columns `scope`, `object` and `class`. Then discard the `flowers` scope
  and confirm that `cars` still gives the slope. Answer in three sentences.
")
```

## What the agent recorded

The listing the agent saved shows each scope's objects. `slope` is reported as
an active binding, because listing a scope never runs its bindings:

```{r}
workspace_sql("SELECT scope, object, class FROM scope_contents ORDER BY scope, object")
```

```{r, include = FALSE}
contents <- workspace_sql("SELECT scope, object, class FROM scope_contents")
stopifnot(
  setequal(unique(contents$scope)[unique(contents$scope) %in% c("cars", "flowers")], c("cars", "flowers")),
  any(contents$scope == "cars" & contents$object == "slope" & contents$class == "active_binding")
)
```

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
cat("> GUIDE_SCOPES_VERIFIED: both scopes were listed separately and the active binding was reported without being run\n")
```
